{"kind":"report","text":"approved at exact head e5ce5aabb3815a62c7c118a1b3f26ae8794ed4b2. Both ratified d19ff5e4 blockers are resolved, and the repair is confined to exactly what was asked.\n\nSCOPE OF THE DELTA. Against the previously reviewed head 4e57b681 the diff is 42 deleted lines in internal/gitstore/gitstore.go and 97 added lines in internal/kernel/kernel_test.go. internal/kernel/kernel.go is byte-identical between the two heads (git diff reports zero lines), so no runtime behaviour moved, and every conclusion from d19ff5e4 about verifier equivalence, the trust root, failure atomicity, fixed Git process counts, PREVIEW and 500k performance, and the crossover carries over to this head unchanged rather than being re-asserted on faith.\n\nBLOCKER 1, ValidatePayloadTree. Gone tree-wide: grep over the whole worktree returns nothing, including tests. The deletion is clean and touches nothing else in the file. No weaker replacement was introduced: the only remaining ls-tree caller is the pre-existing ListFiles helper used by checkpoint reading, and the sole read-side payload-shape and ceiling check is now AuditBatch.PayloadTree at internal/gitstore/audit.go:388, reached only from internal/kernel/kernel.go:1226. One audited path, no shadow.\n\nBLOCKER 2, the listed-route refusal test. TestListedDeltaUsesOnlyEnumeratedObjectIDs refuses reordered, dropped-middle, dropped-last, duplicated and empty lists, and requires the forged-metadata case to produce the identical correct result. It keeps the per-case freshBase() construction, which matters: a successful scan writes its additions into the base dedup map, so a shared base would turn later cases into false refusals and the test would pass for the wrong reason.\n\nTHE MUTATION THAT PREVIOUSLY SURVIVED IS NOW DEAD. Reverting the two audited-metadata reads in deltaScan.accept to the manifest values (audited.Parents to commit.Parents, audited.CommitMetadata to commit) was the mutation that left the entire suite green at 4e57b681. At this head the same mutation fails TestListedDeltaUsesOnlyEnumeratedObjectIDs/forged-metadata-fields, and go test ./... -count=1 exits 1 where it previously exited 0. The head's central security property, that the tail believes the audited object and not the enumeration, now has a regression guard that demonstrably discriminates. Mutation A from the prior round (neutralising VerifySSHSignature in deltaScan.accept) still applies unchanged, since kernel.go did not move. Both mutations were reverted and the worktree verified clean at e5ce5aab.\n\nDisclosure: this test was adapted from the probe I supplied with d19ff5e4, so reading it alone would not be independent review. The evidence I am relying on is the mutation result, which is independent of who wrote the test: the guard was verified by breaking the behaviour and watching it fail, not by inspection.\n\nSPOT-CHECK. Same freshly prepared depth-512 fixture, tail 255: main 05c0ab00 gives 11.577-11.602 s, this head gives 342.4-343.4 ms, a 33.8x improvement with identical correctness digests on every sample and snapshot_source verified_signed_checkpoint_tail throughout. That reproduces the 11.5-11.95 s regression this work was raised against and confirms the hot path did not shift under the deletion. The 50k and 500k rows were not re-measured and did not need to be, because kernel.go is unchanged and the deleted function had no callers; the PREVIEW row (17.6 s to 5.9 s against a 10 s budget) and the FIRST-PRODUCTION row (70.9 s to 57.9 s against a 60 s budget, still load-dominated with about 2 s of margin) stand as measured in d19ff5e4.\n\nGATES at the exact head: go build ./... , go vet ./... , go test ./... -count=1, and go test -race -count=1 over internal/kernel, internal/gitstore and internal/service all pass; 28 package results, no failures.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Layers 1 and 2. Layer 1, internal/gitstore, loses one exported Store method that had no callers; the package is internal, so there are no consumers outside this module, and the layer's described responsibilities in docs/reference/architecture.md do not enumerate Store methods or change. Layer 2, internal/kernel, is unchanged at runtime and gains only test coverage. No contract moves, so architecture.md needs no update in this head.\n\nSECURITY. No defect. The change is strictly surface-reducing: it deletes a payload validator that trusted git ls-tree output and never re-hashed, leaving verification to the audited reader alone, and it adds the guard that pins the listed route to enumerated object ids. Trust boundaries, signature checking, ordering, ceilings and dedup are untouched from the previously approved analysis.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Both prior findings are resolved: the batch/non-batch loadCommit divergence was deleted in the earlier head, and the dead weaker validator is deleted here. Two non-blocking items remain, unchanged and not worth holding this head for: accept still mixes audited.OID and commit.OID, which readObject pins equal so it is cosmetic; and the rotation-to-current-key guard exists in three copies (kernel.go:951, kernel.go:1112, checkpoint.go:493) with no test on any of them, which is pre-existing and deserves its own request rather than a drive-by fix here.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:7994ba104c1bf7232e74e2a01841eb629c0bb8c0","head":"e5ce5aabb3815a62c7c118a1b3f26ae8794ed4b2","verdict":"approved"}}